B. Complete program

import java.io.IOException;

class ExamException extends Exception {
    ExamException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }

    ExamException(String message, Throwable cause) {
        super(message, cause);
    }
}

class SubmissionStorageException extends ExamException {
    SubmissionStorageException(String message, Throwable cause) {
        super(message, cause);
    }
}

class Storage {
    void save(String fileName, long sizeKb)
            throws SubmissionStorageException {

        try {
            if (sizeKb > 500)
                throw new IOException("Disk quota exceeded");

            System.out.println("Saved " + fileName);

        } catch (IOException e) {
            // TODO 1 completed
            throw new SubmissionStorageException(
                "Could not store " + fileName, e
            );
        }
    }
}

public class StorageDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Storage storage = new Storage();

        try {
            storage.save("answer01.pdf", 120);
            storage.save("answer02.pdf", 800);

        } catch (ExamException e) {

            System.out.println("Error: " + e.getMessage());
            System.out.println("Cause: " + e.getCause().getMessage());
        }
    }
}



C. Expected output
Program output
Saved answer01.pdf
Error: Could not store answer02.pdf
Cause: Disk quota exceeded



Explanation:
1. The first file is 120 KB, which is below the 500 KB limit, so it is saved.
2. The second file is 800 KB, which exceeds the limit.
3. An IOException is generated.
4. The catch block wraps it inside SubmissionStorageException.
5. The main method catches the parent ExamException.
6. getMessage() displays the custom message.
7. getCause().getMessage() displays the original IOException message.
